## Why This Is Asked

This question assesses the ability to design a real-time, multi-tenant credit scoring pipeline with strong replay protection, verifiability, and auditability under streaming latency constraints.

## Key Concepts

- **Merkle proofs and verifiable data structures** provide tamper-evident audit trails
- **Per-tenant isolation** ensures strict data residency across Kafka topics
- **Replay protection** via sequence monotonicity and timestamp windows
- **Streaming windowed aggregation** and rollups for real-time computation

## Code Example

```javascript
function verifyDelta(delta, root, proof) {
  const validSequence = lastSequence[delta.tenant_id] < delta.sequence;
  const validSignature = verifySignature(delta.payload, delta.signature, delta.tenant_id);
  const validMerkle = verifyMerkleProof(delta, proof, root);
  return validSequence && validSignature && validMerkle;
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests designing a real-time privacy-preserving federation with streaming updates, focusing on DP budgets, monotonic aggregation, replay protection, and fault tolerance.

## Key Concepts
- Real-time streaming aggregation with privacy guarantees
- Per-device DP budgeting and accounting
- Replay protection and clock skew handling
- Throughput considerations and backpressure in edge-to-cloud pipelines

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-code: validate delta
function processDelta({device_id, ts, delta, nonce, dp_noise}) {
  if (nonce in usedNonces[device_id]) throw 'replay';
  usedNonces[device_id].add(nonce);
  if (ts <= lastTs[device_id]) delta = 0; // monotone
  // apply clipping
  delta = Math.max(-MAX_DELTA, Math.min(MAX_DELTA, delta));
  globalSum += delta + dp_noise;
  budgets[device_id] -= DP_COST;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test privacy budget exhaustion under burst traffic?
- How would you handle device churn and late arrivals without leaking budgets?

